overview:
    I investigated turbulence-induced lift responses across 13 angles of attack from -7˚ to 5˚ and 5–7 turbulence intensity conditions, identifying a nonlinear relationship between angle of attack and lift distribution skewness. Found that eagle models exhibited enhanced vertical acceleration fluctuations under turbulent flow for both symmetric and asymmetric airfoil configurations.

contributions:

- Conducted 15-minute wind tunnel experiments at 80 Hz to quantify turbulence responses of eagle models, analyzing lift distributions and frequency characteristics in MATLAB.
- Investigated turbulence-induced lift responses across 13 angles of attack from -7˚ to 5˚ and 5–7 turbulence intensity conditions, identifying a nonlinear relationship between angle of attack and lift distribution skewness.
- Verified convergence of third-moment estimates using displacement autocorrelation and PDF-integrand analysis, establishing that 15-minute measurement windows provided sufficiently converged results.
- Characterized turbulence intensity with single-wire hot-wire anemometers (5 µm, platinum) at 6kHz and calibrated against simultaneously collected Pitot-tube data.
- Found that eagle models exhibited enhanced vertical acceleration fluctuations under turbulent flow for both symmetric and asymmetric airfoil configurations.
- Conducted experimental validation of mechanical noise, EMI, mounting configuration, and other environmental/setup effects to verify that measured turbulence responses were not dominated by experimental artifacts.

